# A lumpy remainder split into whole quanta and the residue. One row per lumpy layer that states
# its demand, with a single-valued quantum and one unit across demand, nameplate and quantum.
#
# The split
#   `q` is the quantum. The demand fills some whole quanta (`d_*_tooth`) and leaves a residue
#   (`d_*_residue`), the part of the demand no whole quantum meets. `m_*` is the number of whole
#   quanta the remainder spans: the quanta the nameplate holds, less the whole quanta the demand
#   fills. The `m` whole quanta less the residue come to the same figure as the nameplate less the
#   demand, for any demand and any nameplate, range or not.
#
# Read at opposite ends
#   `m` is carried at the remainder's three points, and each pairs the nameplate with the demand at
#   the opposite end, as layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc does: `m_low` pairs `n_low` with
#   `d_high`. The whole quanta the demand fills and its residue are carried at the demand's own
#   three points, because they depend on the demand alone.
#
# The two halves are not claims
#   The residue drops back each time the demand fills another whole quantum, so at a demand's three
#   points it need not come back in order, and the whole quanta jump with it. `crosses_tooth` says
#   where: a demand range that stays within one whole quantum has its residues in order, and one
#   that crosses into the next need not. That is why the schema carries the total and never the
#   split, and why this relation works the split out from the remainder rather than reading
#   something a filer could state.
#
# One unit, a single-valued quantum
#   Counting the whole quanta the demand fills divides the demand by the quantum, which means
#   nothing across units, and is three different divisions for a quantum that is a range. Both are
#   left out rather than guessed at. checks/quantum_unit_mismatch reports the first, and no document
#   here files a quantum that is a range. A single-valued quantum is its own low and high, so `q`
#   is the quantum at every point of the nameplate, the pairing checks/nameplate_not_a_multiple
#   makes point by point.
-- layers/lumpy.sqlc, split into whole quanta and the demand's residue, at opposite ends.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer, l.unit,
       l.quantum_mode AS q,
       l.n_low, l.n_mode, l.n_high,
       l.d_low, l.d_mode, l.d_high,
       floor(l.d_low  / l.quantum_mode) AS d_low_tooth,
       floor(l.d_mode / l.quantum_mode) AS d_mode_tooth,
       floor(l.d_high / l.quantum_mode) AS d_high_tooth,
       mod(l.d_low,  l.quantum_mode)    AS d_low_residue,
       mod(l.d_mode, l.quantum_mode)    AS d_mode_residue,
       mod(l.d_high, l.quantum_mode)    AS d_high_residue,
       l.n_low  / l.quantum_mode - floor(l.d_high / l.quantum_mode) AS m_low,
       l.n_mode / l.quantum_mode - floor(l.d_mode / l.quantum_mode) AS m_mode,
       l.n_high / l.quantum_mode - floor(l.d_low  / l.quantum_mode) AS m_high,
       floor(l.d_low / l.quantum_mode) <> floor(l.d_high / l.quantum_mode) AS crosses_tooth
FROM (
    :compose(layers/lumpy.sqlc)
) l
WHERE l.d_low IS NOT NULL
  AND l.quantum_mode > 0
  AND l.quantum_low = l.quantum_high
  AND l.unit = l.amount_unit
  AND l.quantum_unit = l.amount_unit
